; Rewrite : a row-wise op of a concatenation is the concatenation of the op
; over its parts, `f(cat(a, b), t) = cat(f(a, t[:A]), f(b, t[A:]))`.
; Elementwise ops act on each element alone, so over the rows a
; concatenation takes from a they read a and the same rows of every other
; operand (a row-indexed table like a rotary embedding's included), and
; likewise for b. The result is spelled with the certified concatenation's
; own condition and maps (concat_slices.egg), so it is itself a certified
; concatenation and the next elementwise op pushes through it too; a view
; or a reduction stops the push (their row slices sink instead,
; concat_sink.egg). Where another operand itself carries the rows (a norm's
; statistics, a rotary embedding's rotated half), its two row slices are
; demanded, so they sink to computations over the parts. Same values, same
; rounding: each element is computed by the same op on the same operands.
(rule
  (
    (concat-template ?x ?axis ?split ?condition ?true_map ?false_map ?shape)
    (concat-parts ?x ?axis ?split ?a ?b)
    (= ?y (LogicalExp ?x))
  )
  (
    (union ?y
      (LogicalSelect ?condition
        (LogicalIndexMapApply (LogicalExp ?a) ?true_map ?shape)
        (LogicalIndexMapApply (LogicalExp ?b) ?false_map ?shape)))
  )
  :ruleset main_ruleset
  :name "select/concat_rowwise: Exp of a concatenation is the concatenation of its parts' Exp"
)
(rule
  (
    (concat-template ?x ?axis ?split ?condition ?true_map ?false_map ?shape)
    (concat-parts ?x ?axis ?split ?a ?b)
    (= ?y (LogicalExp2 ?x))
  )
  (
    (union ?y
      (LogicalSelect ?condition
        (LogicalIndexMapApply (LogicalExp2 ?a) ?true_map ?shape)
        (LogicalIndexMapApply (LogicalExp2 ?b) ?false_map ?shape)))
  )
  :ruleset main_ruleset
  :name "select/concat_rowwise: Exp2 of a concatenation is the concatenation of its parts' Exp2"
)
